<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paradiso (Dante)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paradiso is the third and final part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a>, completed shortly before his death in 1321. Guided by Beatrice, Dante ascends through the heavenly spheres to the vision of God. In a letter traditionally attributed to him, Dante dedicated it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cangrande-i-della-scala">Cangrande I della Scala</a>, lord of Verona, who had sheltered him in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paradiso-dante.jpg" alt="Paradiso (Dante)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firenze,_commedia_di_dante,_paradiso,_1325-50_(con_intregrazioni_dei_canti_VI-XXXIII_del_xix_sec),_pluteo_40.15,_c_5v,_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dates of composition are uncertain, but research by Giorgio Petrocchi suggests that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> began planning the Paradiso while revising the Inferno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purgatorio">Purgatorio</a> between 1313 and 1317, and it was probably not published until after his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a> claimed that the poem was at first thought unfinished until Dante's sons found its last thirteen cantos among his papers. Robert Hollander points to small contradictions absent from the first two parts and suggests that Dante may not have finished revising it. Like them it has thirty-three cantos, but it has far fewer speakers, under twenty against over fifty in each earlier part, and longer speeches with more philosophical discussion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The journey begins in the Earthly Paradise at the top of Mount Purgatory at noon on a Wednesday in 1300, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, and lasts about twenty-four hours. Beatrice leads <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> up through nine concentric spheres, as in the medieval geocentric cosmos derived from Ptolemy: the Moon, Mercury, Venus, the Sun, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, the Fixed Stars and the Primum Mobile, beyond which lies the non-material Empyrean. Where Hell and Purgatory were ordered by sins, Heaven is ordered by the four cardinal and three theological virtues. All the blessed live with God in the Empyrean, but they are shown to Dante in the spheres as a sign.</p><p>In the Moon he meets Piccarda Donati, in Mercury the Emperor Justinian, who recounts the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and in the Sun Thomas Aquinas among twelve lights of the wise; Aquinas tells the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-of-assisi">Francis of Assisi</a> and Bonaventure that of Dominic. In Mars his ancestor Cacciaguida, a crusader, foretells his exile. The just rulers of Jupiter form an imperial eagle, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Ripheus the Trojan. In the Fixed Stars Peter, James and John examine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> on faith, hope and love, and Peter denounces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>.</p><p>In the Primum Mobile Dante sees God as a point of light ringed by nine orders of angels. In the Empyrean the saved sit as petals of a vast rose; Beatrice takes her place there, and Saint Bernard guides Dante on and prays to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> for him. At last Dante sees God as three circles in one space, with the human form of Christ within them, and his will is turned by the love that moves the sun and the other stars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paradiso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cangrande I, lord from 1311 to 1329, conquered Vicenza, Padua and Treviso and hosted the exiled Dante, who dedicated the <b>Paradiso</b> to him.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a></li></ul></section>
